WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.360 --> 00:00:02.450
Hello there!

00:00:02.450 --> 00:00:07.670
Are you tired of using tokenizers for your
language transformers?

00:00:07.670 --> 00:00:12.629
Highly likely you’re not, because usually,
these lines from the huggingface library do

00:00:12.629 --> 00:00:18.770
the heavy lifting for you, so you usually
do not care about tokenization.

00:00:18.770 --> 00:00:24.990
But tokenization, is a pre-processing step,
therefore it is rigid and not adapted to your

00:00:24.990 --> 00:00:27.300
application nor your data.

00:00:27.300 --> 00:00:33.280
But wait no more: Charformer is here, enabling
end-to-end language transformers, that do

00:00:33.280 --> 00:00:37.570
not require tokenization anymore!

00:00:37.570 --> 00:00:39.640
Ms. Coffee Bean, are we cool?

00:00:39.640 --> 00:00:45.890
You know what happened in our last video and
I only wanted to know if we are ok now.

00:00:45.890 --> 00:00:47.120
Yes?

00:00:47.120 --> 00:00:48.350
Cool.

00:00:48.350 --> 00:00:52.540
Tokenization is that part in pre-processing
that is done to text

00:00:52.540 --> 00:00:56.070
before throwing it into your favourite language
model, say BERT.

00:00:56.070 --> 00:01:04.579
It’s a pre-processing step, so it is independent
of the later training process, but it's crucial

00:01:04.579 --> 00:01:06.530
for most transformers.

00:01:06.530 --> 00:01:11.580
Without tokenization, each word of the English
vocabulary would have to be assigned a unique

00:01:11.580 --> 00:01:15.990
vector on the input side
and, when predicting the masked word in the

00:01:15.990 --> 00:01:21.210
output, the choice would have to consider
the whole vocabulary.

00:01:21.210 --> 00:01:27.119
And if you believe that the English vocabulary
that one can find in a dictionary, is already

00:01:27.119 --> 00:01:34.979
huge, think about how it can grow even faster
with all words that are not in a dictionary,

00:01:34.979 --> 00:01:40.670
like compound words created on the fly by
someone as a stylistic figure.

00:01:40.670 --> 00:01:43.640
Or all possible typos, right?

00:01:43.640 --> 00:01:50.740
A mistyped word is also a word and should
have a way to be processed by a language model.

00:01:50.740 --> 00:01:58.020
This is where tokenization comes in, to at
least partially, solve this problem by creating

00:01:58.020 --> 00:02:01.890
a smaller vocabulary of word building blocks.

00:02:01.890 --> 00:02:09.080
This hopes to cover as much of the dictionary
as possible, including unexpected words.

00:02:09.080 --> 00:02:15.890
Therefore, tokenizers first segment the sentence
into words (harder for some languages than

00:02:15.890 --> 00:02:21.690
for others, looking at you, Chinese, Thai
and Korean, because you do not have whitespace

00:02:21.690 --> 00:02:22.920
separation).

00:02:22.920 --> 00:02:29.080
Then the words are parsed into sub-words,
so that even though supposedly the dictionary

00:02:29.080 --> 00:02:34.790
does not have the word “pre-processing”
in it, and therefore no vector representation

00:02:34.790 --> 00:02:42.310
to associate with it, the tokenizer can divide
the word into “pre” and “processing”,

00:02:42.310 --> 00:02:46.940
supposing that “pre” and “processing”
are already in the vocabulary and have a vector

00:02:46.940 --> 00:02:47.940
association.

00:02:47.940 --> 00:02:54.470
And this works great in very regular kinds
of words that can be decomposed into known

00:02:54.470 --> 00:03:00.849
blocks, but does not solve the problem of
noisy text, full of typos where the possibilities

00:03:00.849 --> 00:03:07.550
of mistyping are so many, that a fall-back
to a known subword is either impossible or

00:03:07.550 --> 00:03:09.480
leads to misunderstandings.

00:03:09.480 --> 00:03:14.990
So tokenization, being so rigid can become
a problem, an even bigger one when working

00:03:14.990 --> 00:03:21.390
with multiple languages where the out-of-vocabulary
problem becomes even greater with every additional

00:03:21.390 --> 00:03:23.880
language the model has to handle.

00:03:23.880 --> 00:03:30.690
Even more, the downside of tokenization is
that on downstream applications, you are kind-of

00:03:30.690 --> 00:03:35.971
forced to use the same tokenizer as the model
was trained with, because the trained model

00:03:35.971 --> 00:03:40.590
is very much dependent on the tokenization
it is used to.

00:03:40.590 --> 00:03:45.720
Even though your task might benefit from other
tokenizations, you now must stick with the

00:03:45.720 --> 00:03:47.000
initial ones.

00:03:47.000 --> 00:03:50.780
No, you're not allowed to see other tokenizers,
Ms. Coffee Bean!

00:03:50.780 --> 00:03:55.129
Here is where Charformer comes to save the
day:

00:03:55.129 --> 00:04:00.650
what if you would have a completely end-to-end
language transformer that does not require

00:04:00.650 --> 00:04:07.239
this tokenization pre-processing step,
and takes in the text as-it-is: in characters

00:04:07.239 --> 00:04:13.940
and dynamically computes fitting representations
for your task and data on the fly?

00:04:13.940 --> 00:04:19.289
But Ms. Coffee Bean wants to know, why do
we need the Charformer in the first place?

00:04:19.289 --> 00:04:24.979
What if we would take in the characters,
give them all an initial vector representation

00:04:24.979 --> 00:04:30.009
and throw them like that into the transformer
and... boom?

00:04:30.009 --> 00:04:31.009
Problem solved.

00:04:31.009 --> 00:04:34.259
Well, Ms. Coffee Bean… it is not that easy.

00:04:34.259 --> 00:04:37.360
On the one hand, it is exactly what related
work does.

00:04:37.360 --> 00:04:41.069
But that also means that
the sequence length for the transformer would

00:04:41.069 --> 00:04:44.270
be the same as the number of characters.

00:04:44.270 --> 00:04:50.050
This can be a lot, which increases computation
and memory complexity.

00:04:50.050 --> 00:04:51.340
Significantly.

00:04:51.340 --> 00:04:58.580
Therefore the Charformer enables faster processing
of raw character inputs, by dynamically tokenizing

00:04:58.580 --> 00:05:05.939
the characters into sub-words, then feeding
the tokenizations into a transformer and training

00:05:05.939 --> 00:05:11.199
everything jointly, so tokenizer and transformer
at once.

00:05:11.199 --> 00:05:16.180
Hereby it achieves the flexibility of other
character-level models, but does not blow

00:05:16.180 --> 00:05:21.520
up the sequence length because it learns a
tokenizer which reduces the sequence length,

00:05:21.520 --> 00:05:22.930
on the fly.

00:05:22.930 --> 00:05:25.940
Tokenization with gradient shagabum, nice!

00:05:25.940 --> 00:05:30.719
So great, now that we know what happens high-level,
let’s see under the hood.

00:05:30.719 --> 00:05:35.379
Every time the authors say “character”,
they mean "byte", because this is how they

00:05:35.379 --> 00:05:39.539
encode each character,
allowing them to work with many languages,

00:05:39.539 --> 00:05:44.069
where all language characters are all encoded
in this byte space.

00:05:44.069 --> 00:05:50.210
So the idea is to have your input, which is
a sequence of L characters, each encoded by

00:05:50.210 --> 00:05:53.460
d-dimensional vectors, here 8-dimensional
bytes.

00:05:53.460 --> 00:06:00.360
And the goal with this learned tokenization,
called Gradient-Based Subword Tokenization,

00:06:00.360 --> 00:06:06.149
or short GBST
so GBST... is to find suitable representations

00:06:06.149 --> 00:06:12.650
of spans of characters to then pass them on
together to the transformer.

00:06:12.650 --> 00:06:16.449
Here in the visualization, a vector is denoted
just by x_1.

00:06:16.449 --> 00:06:23.289
For this, GBST takes the character sequence
and their byte representation, and forms character

00:06:23.289 --> 00:06:25.849
blocks, a little like n-grams.

00:06:25.849 --> 00:06:32.009
So first are the 1-grams, there the block
size is 1, then we have the two-character

00:06:32.009 --> 00:06:35.929
blocks, 3 character blocks and so on.

00:06:35.929 --> 00:06:39.339
And GBST computes a representation for each
block

00:06:39.339 --> 00:06:45.150
by mean pooling the representations of each
character in each block.

00:06:45.150 --> 00:06:49.750
Ms. Coffee Bean, didn’t we say that we want
to reduce the sequence length?

00:06:49.750 --> 00:06:56.319
Now we have 4 sequences instead of one and
by mean pooling we lost the order of the individual

00:06:56.319 --> 00:07:01.110
characters for block sizes bigger than 1,
what are we even doing?

00:07:01.110 --> 00:07:08.459
Aaah, the recipe goes further: In order to
avoid losing the order by mean pooling the

00:07:08.459 --> 00:07:15.800
authors add: positional embeddings!!, which
are encoding the order of characters in the

00:07:15.800 --> 00:07:16.800
blocks.

00:07:16.800 --> 00:07:18.469
Okay, problem solved!

00:07:18.469 --> 00:07:21.340
Are we going to reduce the length now?

00:07:21.340 --> 00:07:26.490
Note that for bigger block sizes, we have
fewer vectors than the number of characters,

00:07:26.490 --> 00:07:30.939
so the mean pooled representation of each
block is repeated now,

00:07:30.939 --> 00:07:35.360
such that now every character has one vector
assigned to it.

00:07:35.360 --> 00:07:40.969
So here x1 equals x2, because x2 is just copied
from x1.

00:07:40.969 --> 00:07:42.300
For the 2-grams.

00:07:42.300 --> 00:07:43.300
Or the 2-blocks.

00:07:43.300 --> 00:07:48.110
Now the idea is to get a new, better representation
for each character.

00:07:48.110 --> 00:07:53.879
One way to do it is by hard selection, by
choosing one representation from all the 4

00:07:53.879 --> 00:07:56.300
possibilities
and to throw away the rest.

00:07:56.300 --> 00:08:03.300
But the authors proposed soft selection,
by a weighted sum of all block representations.

00:08:03.300 --> 00:08:08.250
The weights here, in other words the scorings,
are learned.

00:08:08.250 --> 00:08:14.250
It is the only part in this tokenizer that
is learned through SGD on the task and its

00:08:14.250 --> 00:08:15.250
data.

00:08:15.250 --> 00:08:20.209
It kind-of makes sense that this part is learned,
unlike the other parts where mean pooling

00:08:20.209 --> 00:08:25.899
is hard-coded, because for getting sensible
importance scores from the vectors, they first

00:08:25.899 --> 00:08:27.939
have to be adapted to the data.

00:08:27.939 --> 00:08:33.669
So we now have a new representation for each
character being the weighted sum of the block

00:08:33.669 --> 00:08:35.529
representations.

00:08:35.529 --> 00:08:38.219
So wait a minute: what has just happened here?

00:08:38.219 --> 00:08:45.980
Well, in simple words, GBST basically asks:
what does each character need from its fellow

00:08:45.980 --> 00:08:48.670
characters to inform its representation?

00:08:48.670 --> 00:08:54.830
And what if each character can choose between
1-gram information, 2-gram, 3-gram and so

00:08:54.830 --> 00:08:55.940
on?

00:08:55.940 --> 00:09:02.830
Or even better: each character has the option
to do a combination of 1-gram, 2-gram ... information!

00:09:02.830 --> 00:09:07.639
In order to determine how much they should
be combined, a transformation is learned to

00:09:07.639 --> 00:09:09.470
determine these importances.

00:09:09.470 --> 00:09:13.740
The gradient updates will tell the linear
transformation responsible for computing the

00:09:13.740 --> 00:09:15.959
scores how much scoring is needed.

00:09:15.959 --> 00:09:22.399
It is almost like attention, with the only
difference that each block position is uninformed

00:09:22.399 --> 00:09:26.670
about what the other positions do and how
they combine things.

00:09:26.670 --> 00:09:33.930
So now, (similarly to the MLP-Mixer or the
FNet which is the Fourier Transform Transformer),

00:09:33.930 --> 00:09:39.480
we have first mixed the characters among each
other by neighbourhood,

00:09:39.480 --> 00:09:43.399
then we mix again, but this time orthogonal
to the first mixing.

00:09:43.399 --> 00:09:49.500
I honestly did not expect to find any parallel
between the MLP-Mixer and the Charformer,

00:09:49.500 --> 00:09:50.610
but here it is.

00:09:50.610 --> 00:09:55.209
And now we have as many representations as
characters, yay!

00:09:55.209 --> 00:09:56.970
Well, not yay.

00:09:56.970 --> 00:10:03.800
We started with L characters and their raw
representations, now we still have L character

00:10:03.800 --> 00:10:10.029
representations, which are now more informed,
yes, but didn’t we say we want to reduce

00:10:10.029 --> 00:10:11.029
sequence length?

00:10:11.029 --> 00:10:15.910
Yes, we did, and this is the final step of
GBST:

00:10:15.910 --> 00:10:17.350
downsampling.

00:10:17.350 --> 00:10:24.310
These new L learnt character representations
are downsampled again by a mean-pooling operation

00:10:24.310 --> 00:10:26.459
over neighbouring representations.

00:10:26.459 --> 00:10:30.290
And this is hard-coded, not learned.

00:10:30.290 --> 00:10:37.019
The only learnable step in GBST tokenizer
is the scoring of the character n-gram representations.

00:10:37.019 --> 00:10:38.019
Wow.

00:10:38.019 --> 00:10:41.450
So is this tokenization procedure elegant,
what do you think?

00:10:41.450 --> 00:10:46.590
Well, we honestly, don’t know,
does it *have* to be elegant?

00:10:46.590 --> 00:10:50.660
Mixing is all you need, the mixing does not
even have to be learned,

00:10:50.660 --> 00:10:55.850
as we have seen in the Fourier Transform Transformer
and in other previous videos.

00:10:55.850 --> 00:11:02.269
Any mixing and that’s what these authors
also do, often hard-coded, seems to work with

00:11:02.269 --> 00:11:03.269
transformers.

00:11:03.269 --> 00:11:07.720
So, was this long road of character representation
mixing worth it?

00:11:07.720 --> 00:11:09.670
Yes, it kind-of was.

00:11:09.670 --> 00:11:15.630
These new representations from the GBST tokenizer
are ready to be fed into your favourite transformers.

00:11:15.630 --> 00:11:20.820
Especially because very little aspects in
GBST are learned, optimization will not waste

00:11:20.820 --> 00:11:22.990
a lot of time on the tokenizer part.

00:11:22.990 --> 00:11:29.079
Also, the tokenizer reduces the sequence length,
making the transformer downstream also faster.

00:11:29.079 --> 00:11:34.399
I guess you can convince yourself of the performance
of the Charformer on the tables in the paper

00:11:34.399 --> 00:11:35.540
(linked below).

00:11:35.540 --> 00:11:41.430
Charformer is sometimes superior, sometimes
on-par with state-of the art character-level

00:11:41.430 --> 00:11:42.850
models on GLUE.

00:11:42.850 --> 00:11:49.180
Also, this approach works well in multilingual
settings and also on noisy text, where hard-coded

00:11:49.180 --> 00:11:51.290
tokenization is brittle usually.

00:11:51.290 --> 00:11:56.399
This good performance is somewhat expected,
because now the tokenization adapts to your

00:11:56.399 --> 00:12:01.980
dataset and task while not blowing up your
sequence length.

00:12:01.980 --> 00:12:07.230
And 0.1% state-of-the art up-and-down is less
important for Ms. Coffee Bean than the flexibility

00:12:07.230 --> 00:12:11.980
of the Charformer on all the other applications
other than GLUE.

00:12:11.980 --> 00:12:18.629
So yeah, I guess that this paper, even though
framed differently from the MLP-Mixer, the

00:12:18.629 --> 00:12:23.540
transformer replacing self-attention with
convolutions or the Fourier Transform transformer,

00:12:23.540 --> 00:12:30.029
is again showing that mixing representations
somehow, almost anyhow works, as long as you

00:12:30.029 --> 00:12:33.430
have an transformer-like architecture in general
downstream.

00:12:33.430 --> 00:12:38.680
And yes, by this we mean that self-attention
is not essential for transformers,

00:12:38.680 --> 00:12:41.740
see this video linked below for more about
that.

00:12:41.740 --> 00:12:47.769
Hmm… but this whole mixing, right? this
reminds us of something, Ms. Coffee Bean.

00:12:47.769 --> 00:12:49.240
What was it?

00:12:49.240 --> 00:12:50.839
Aaah, yes!

00:12:50.839 --> 00:12:54.660
You know, those times of feature engineering
long gone?

00:12:54.660 --> 00:12:57.769
Now I guess it’s time for mixing engineering.

00:12:57.769 --> 00:13:00.069
Cheers to that!

00:13:00.069 --> 00:13:02.339
Ms. Coffee Bean, what are you doing?

00:13:02.339 --> 00:13:03.339
Ms. Coffee Bean?

00:13:03.339 --> 00:13:04.339
Noo…!

00:13:04.339 --> 00:13:05.339
I thought we were friend again!

00:13:05.339 --> 00:13:07.310
No, Ms. Coffee Bean!?

00:13:07.310 --> 00:13:09.970
Okay bye!

